 "literal": "Roaring white waterfall because of bearing, color having changed, / Tillai tree like lusterless with matted hair, / dense leaf tāḷi plant he who plucks indeed, / house moving about gentle peacock binding / word net hunter he became formerly indeed.",
 "translation": "Because he stood under the roaring white waterfall, his complexion has changed. With lusterless matted hair resembling the tillai tree, he now plucks the dense-leaved tāḷi herb. Yet, in the past, he was a hunter who cast a net of words to captivate the gentle peacocks who moved about his house.",
 "modern": "ஒலிக்கும் வெண்மையான அருவிநீரைத் தன் மேல் ஏற்றுக்கொண்டதால் தன் உடலின் நிறம் மாறி, தில்லை மரத்தைப் போன்ற பொலிவிழந்த சடைமுடியோடு, அடர்ந்த இலைகளைக் கொண்ட தாளி கீரையைப் பறித்து உண்பவனாக அவன் இப்போது இருக்கிறான். ஆனால், முன்னொரு காலத்தில் அவன் தன் இல்லத்தில் உலவும் இளமையான மயில் போன்ற பெண்களைத் தன் இன்சொல் என்னும் வலையால் கட்டிப்போடும் வேட்டுவனாக இருந்தான்.",
 "explanation": "In this poem, the poet Māṟpittiyār observes a man who has renounced his domestic life to become an ascetic. The poet contrasts the man's present state of severe penance with his luxurious and romantic past. Now, his skin is bleached from bathing under cold mountain waterfalls, his hair is dry and matted like the branches of a tillai tree, and he gathers wild greens for his simple meals. Formerly, however, he was a charming householder who used sweet words as a net to captivate his gentle, peacock-like wives. This stark contrast beautifully illustrates the theme of 'tāpata vākai', which celebrates the greatness and self-control of an ascetic.",
